**Read:** [Chemical Reactor Analysis and Design Fundamentals — Rawlings and Ekerdt](../sources/Chemical-Reactor-Analysis-Rawlings-Ekerdt.lingua#r_7YQAV27Y3J0B537Z6K5HDN8H0A), the complete nonideal-reactor and residence-time-distribution chapter; [Chemical and Biological Reaction Engineering — MIT 10.37](../sources/Reaction-Engineering-MIT-10-37.lingua#r_67AEXD9K1DJ46SV942PAZHZ4BZ), all nonideal-flow, tracer and mixing lecture/problem material; [LearnChemE — chemical-process self-study modules](../sources/LearnChemE-Process-Self-Study.lingua#r_4KM1THP1QA2SZ5JJ9E1HSMN529), the complete relevant reactor/residence teaching modules.

Residence-time distribution describes how long sampled material spends in a system under a specified tracer/flow experiment. A normalized exit-age density E(t) integrates to one; its mean and variance summarize part of the distribution. Injection duration, detector dynamics, sampling, tracer interactions and changing flow can distort the inferred result.

Different internal flow patterns can produce similar distributions, and the same residence distribution can lead to different outcomes for nonlinear reactions when micromixing differs. Separate a tracer-flow inference from a complete reacting/thermal model. Dead volumes, bypass, recirculation and catalyst or phase residence can require additional observations.

For n equal ideal stirred tanks with total mean residence τ, the ideal distribution has variance τ²/n. Increasing n narrows that distribution toward ideal plug flow, but does not magically correct heat transfer or establish the right reaction mechanism. The original consecutive-reaction cascade demonstrates one limited effect of changing residence history.

**Work:** analyze a complete primary tracer study with the actual injection and measurement method. Independently check normalization, mean/variance and recovery, compare competing flow models and propagate uncertainty into product predictions. Explain which further observation could discriminate models that the tracer curve alone cannot distinguish.

**Continue:** [Distinguish a thermal steady state, its stability and the trajectory reaching it](Nonisothermal-steady-states-stability-and-transients.lingua#r_20YMRWMPPJ12WQBT3M9ZSBKAZK).

